From 2b005f2c82f21d670c6bb4e2f30886b438e6e840 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rou <shamay1030@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 13:07:05 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 12/27] Let the host say what to reuse, not how to lay it out

pic_link arrived as an untyped dict, so a misspelt key was accepted in
silence and three host builders had drifted into three different field
sets. The plan-file benchmarks sent block_ranges, imp_indices and
mutable_start_token; the live adapter sent framing policy and equivalence
evidence; the Terminal-Bench agent sent neither. One of those paths
produced the performance number and another produced the correctness
number, so "it is one system" was an assumption.

_prepare_link already overwrote every physical field a host sent, so this
does not change what executes. It changes what is sayable. The contract
splits into what the host decides -- which blocks, in what order, under
what repair policy, and when it owns the chat template exactly which
tokens -- and what this process derives from its own registry: block
ranges, global offsets, the mutable boundary, sparse-repair positions.

A host that sends a derived field is now refused rather than silently
overridden, because a stale imp_indices in a saved plan file read like it
was still steering the run. SGLANG_PIC_ALLOW_LEGACY_LINK_FIELDS=1 discards
them instead, for replaying a plan that cannot be regenerated; the
resolved plan is identical either way, which is the point.

Unknown fields raise. The five that reached the server and were read by
nothing -- selected_order, expected_cache_keys, framing_policy,
separator_token_ids, equivalence_validation -- are host evidence and
belong beside the request, not in it. The runtime verifies the token hash
framing produced rather than the policy that produced it, which is the
stronger check.

ResolvedLinkPlan names what was previously only a mutated dict, so an
equivalence test has something to compare. The warmup payload loses four
attention-policy strings it was carrying and nothing read.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
